From: Adam Hillson <598804+BadAd84@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] exl3: TF_EXL3_DEC_ORDER launches each k slice's blocks before the items

Our fork `ai-engine`, ref `fork-ssh/ovn-up`, commit 46b46550f "exl3:
TF_EXL3_DEC_ORDER launches each k slice's blocks before the items". Hand
ported: the fork's two hunks do not apply, because the pin has moved
GroupedArgs and grouped_body out of exl3_experts.cu into exl3_grouped.cuh and
our entry points carry a fourth template argument.

The grouped decode kernel's block order decides what the expert stream has in
flight. With the items fastest, which is today, one item's 128-column blocks
are spread across the round. With TF_EXL3_DEC_ORDER=1 each k slice's blocks are
issued together, so consecutive blocks read trellis regions that were fetched
together. The fork measured plus 1 to 3 per cent decode on the MiaAI recipe
(its PR 95); nothing here measures it.

Unset is byte-for-byte today's launch, by construction. ord defaults to 0 at
every producer: decOrder returns 0 unless the variable is exactly "1", the
entry points then take blockIdx.x as u and blockIdx.y as nblock exactly as
before, and the grid keeps x = maxu, y = n / 128. With ord 1 the two axes swap
in the same pair of places, so each block still gets the same (u, nblock) pair
it would have got, only from a different blockIdx. The tiles, lanes and mma
order inside grouped_body do not see ord at all.

It completes 0019-kda-chunked, the patch that owns our exl3_experts.cu and
exl3_grouped.cuh lines.

Differences from the fork's lines, all forced by our file shapes:

- GroupedArgs lives in zig/kernels/cuda/exl3_grouped.cuh here, so `ord` is
  appended to the int list there, not in exl3_experts.cu. The struct's size
  does not change: ten pointers and eleven ints pad to 128 bytes already, and
  twelve ints is still 128.
- our three entry points pass GROUPED_LOAD as a fourth template argument (the
  sm_120 load-ahead, patch 0019), which the fork's lines do not have.
- the fork also edits tf_exl3_grouped_p0, p1 and p2, the TF_EXL3_PIPE walkers.
  This tree has no such kernels and no pipe() on the host, so those three lines
  are left out. That is why our launch call keeps `e.grouped[...]` where the
  fork's reads `if (pipe()) e.grouped_pipe[...] else e.grouped[...]`.
- decOrder() caches its read in a file-scope optional instead of calling
  getenv every time. The fork's code calls decOrder() twice a launch and the
  two grouped launches run once a layer a step, so on a 92-layer TP=3 decode
  round that is several hundred environment scans a step on the default path,
  which would show up in the very A/B the knob exists for. The cached value is
  the same value; the fork uses the same idiom for TF_EXL3_SPLITK.
  Its doc comment is one line, which this repo's lean gate requires.
- zig/tests/cuda/contract_exl3_grouped.zig builds a GroupedArgs by hand for the
  contract's control kernels (contract_controls.cu's five tf_ctl_exl3_grouped_*
  entries take the same struct). Its comment says it is in launch.zig add
  order, so it gains the ord argument, 0, which is what those kernels want:
  they pass blockIdx.x and y straight through and the harness builds its own
  grid. The fork left its copy of this file one field short.

Not compiled locally: zig/kernels/cuda/exl3_grouped.cuh and
zig/kernels/cuda/exl3_experts.cu are CUDA, and zig/tests/cuda/
contract_exl3_grouped.zig belongs to the GPU test binary. The host gates build
none of the three. zig/src/cuda/exl3_experts.zig is host code and is built.

Where this meets the pending item-plan patch (work/p41/0042-exl3-decode-items.
patch), measured rather than guessed. `git apply --check` of that patch takes
the stack without this lane cleanly, and on top of this lane it reports:

  zig/src/cuda/exl3_experts.zig      hunks 3 to 7 at offset 11 (decOrder), then
                                     "patch failed at 204", the gate and up
                                     launch; the down launch behind it is never
                                     reached
  zig/src/families/glm/cuda_exl3.zig "patch failed at 204": its last hunk
                                     appends the items test after the file's
                                     last test, and patch 02 of this lane added
                                     a test there
  zig/tests/cuda/main.zig            hunks at offset 1 and 2, no conflict
  zig/tests/cuda/glm_exl3_items_tests.zig  new file, no conflict

So two hunks, both mechanical. The launch pair needs one grid expression a
launch that takes the item plan's nx or this patch's swap, something like
`if (ord == 1) w / 128 else nx`, with the ord add after skip_a; the test is a
plain append past both. Nothing else of the two overlaps: the item plan's other
hunks are in scratch sizing and the epilogue, this one is in the entry points,
the struct and decOrder.

Compiled here: exl3_experts and contract_controls build clean on nvcc 13.3 at
sm_121 both before and after this patch, with the build's own flags, and
zig build compile-cuda-tests (rc=0) covers the contract harness change.

What a GPU run has to show. First that unset changes nothing: a reply digest
equal to the build before this patch. The construction argument above predicts
it (exl3_grouped.cuh reads no blockIdx or gridDim of its own, so the swap is a
relabelling) and the compile says only that it builds, not what it computes.
Then the knob itself, TF_EXL3_DEC_ORDER=1: the same reply digest again (the
values cannot change, only the block order), and the routed stage's time
against its baseline. The fork gates it against 13.94 ms for that stage. Both
grouped launches take the knob, so a run that only measures the gate and up
projection misses half of it.

Re-seated on v1.2.7 (93a2e93ecebff8aa52b88e58c5dd222516e69c5f, from pin 9c9bf542) by the 2026-11-12 re-seat (work/reseat/DECISIONS.md):
- lean_check.py's DIRECT_GETENV: decOrder() read std.c.getenv("TF_EXL3_DEC_ORDER"); it is now a .cuda flag row read once through environment.value (space-trimmed 0 or 1, refused text read as off, as before any text but 1 was).
- exl3_grouped.cuh and exl3_experts.cu: edc16dfe9 added one line beside the entry points; the ord argument applies as the fold has it.
- platform/environment_test.zig's `valid` table gains TF_EXL3_DEC_ORDER's two forms: its coverage test requires every row outside .glm and .dsv41 there (the first gate run of the full series failed that test without them).
- source: work/fold/0033-exl3-dec-order.patch

